From 8f4e51e853d055c4b1106fd8b8b0fd4ae4b76faa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:15:15 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] NSCalendar: implement the 10.9 API

NSCalendar had none of the methods macOS added in 10.9 apart from
-component:fromDate: and the -getEra:... family: -startOfDayForDate:,
-isDate:inSameDayAsDate:, -isDateInToday:/Yesterday:/Tomorrow:/Weekend:,
-rangeOfWeekendStartDate:interval:containingDate:,
-nextWeekendStartDate:interval:options:afterDate:,
-components:fromDateComponents:toDateComponents:options:,
-dateWithEra:year:month:day:hour:minute:second:nanosecond:,
-dateWithEra:yearForWeekOfYear:weekOfYear:weekday:...,
-dateByAddingUnit:value:toDate:options:,
-dateBySettingUnit:value:ofDate:options:,
-dateBySettingHour:minute:second:ofDate:options:,
-date:matchesComponents:, -isDate:equalToDate:toUnitGranularity:,
-compareDate:toDate:toUnitGranularity:, the three -nextDateAfterDate:...
methods, -enumerateDatesStartingAfterDate:matchingComponents:options:
usingBlock: and -componentsInTimeZone:fromDate: were all missing, as was
-minimumRangeOfUnit: (only a misspelt -minimumRangeofUnit: existed, kept
now as a cover for it).  Every one of them is added here.

Writing them turned up faults in what was there:

- NSCalendarIdentifierISO8601 (and NSISO8601Calendar) was the empty
  string, and three other identifiers were not the names ICU and macOS
  use: "ethiopic", "islamic-tbla" and "islamic-umalqura" were spelt
  "ethiopic-amete-mihret", "islamic-tabular" and "islamic-umm-al-qura",
  so ICU silently gave a Gregorian calendar for each.  The ISO 8601
  calendar now has Monday weeks of at least four days.
- +calendarWithIdentifier: and -initWithCalendarIdentifier: accepted any
  string; an identifier ICU does not know now gives nil, as on macOS.
- -copy of a calendar returned the receiver, although a calendar is
  mutable, and a real copy lost the first weekday and minimum days.
  -[NSDateComponents setCalendar:] now copies, as the property on macOS
  does, so changing a calendar does not change components holding it.
- -rangeOfUnit:startDate:interval:forDate: took the end of a day as the
  start plus a day on the clock, so a day without a midnight (Sao Paulo,
  2018-11-04) was 24 hours, and with two midnights (Havana, 2023-11-05)
  it began at the second.  Units are now measured from the first moment
  of one to the first moment of the next; a repeated wall time resolves
  to its first instance everywhere, as on macOS.  Quarters and week-based
  years are handled too.
- Nanoseconds were milliseconds: -components:fromDate: truncated to the
  millisecond, -getHour:minute:second:nanosecond:fromDate: multiplied
  milliseconds by 1000, and adding or setting nanoseconds dropped the
  part below a millisecond.
- -components:fromDate: ignored NSCalendarUnitCalendar and
  NSCalendarUnitTimeZone; -components:fromDate:toDate:options: returned
  nil whenever NSCalendarUnitEra was asked for, since ICU cannot take a
  difference of eras.
- -ordinalityOfUnit:inUnit:forDate: always returned 0.
- -isValidDate and -isValidDateInCalendar: were YES for anything that
  made a date at all, 30 February and 24:00 included.  A date is now
  valid when it has every component given.
- -valueForComponent: answered 0 for a unit not stored rather than
  NSDateComponentUndefined; NSDateComponents had no -isEqual:, -hash or
  -isLeapMonth.
- -dateFromComponents: used a calendar without the receiver's first
  weekday and minimum days, so week-based components ignored them.

The searches follow the rules in Apple's header, and their results were
checked against macOS 15 case by case: units smaller than the largest one
given take their smallest values; a missing day of the month gives the
start of the next day (NSCalendarMatchNextTime), or the next or previous
day at the starting date's time (the ...PreservingSmallerUnits options);
a time skipped by daylight saving gives the moment the clock resumes, the
time moved on by the gap, or the time moved back by it; of two instances
of a repeated time the first is taken unless NSCalendarMatchLast is
given; strict searches go on through the years up to a limit.  Searches
backwards return the latest match before the date.  Here macOS is not
consistent with itself and the patch does not follow it: going back from
Friday the 15th at 10:45 it skips that day for "day 15" or "weekday 6"
but not for "weekday 6, hour 9", and gives 2023-03-03 for "month 3".
Equally, macOS compares dates to the granularity of a quarter as it does
days; here a quarter is a quarter.

Weekends come from ICU's data for the locale's region (Saturday and
Sunday in the US, Friday and Saturday in Israel).

Two new tests in Tests/base/NSCalendar hold the macOS results as tables:
features-10-9.m (start of day in Sao Paulo, Havana, Apia and New York,
weekends, components and nanoseconds, differences, granularity,
ordinality, identifiers, NSDateComponents validity and equality) and
nextDate.m (139 searches through daylight saving gaps and repeats, short
months and leap days, forwards and backwards, with every matching
option, and the enumeration, -dateBySetting... and -date:matches...
methods).  Without this change they fail; with it the 503 new tests and
the existing NSCalendar, NSCalendarDate, NSDateComponents, NSDate,
NSDateFormatter, NSLocale, NSTimeZone and NSKeyedArchiver tests pass.

Not done: the calendar's symbol properties (eraSymbols, monthSymbols and
so on), NSCalendarDayChangedNotification, and macOS 15's
NSCalendarUnitDayOfYear.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
